{
  "configuration_id": "config_004",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_007 (rna_only, pca_n2000, singleprofile) achieved 0.485 acc, and config_009 (multimodal, pca_n2000, resnet50, concat, singleprofile) improved to 0.527 acc, confirming that adding image modality helps. However, both experiments use pca_n2000—a linear dimensionality reduction—as the RNA encoder. It is unclear whether this linear representation is a bottleneck for the RNA branch in the multimodal setting. Question: config_004 (multimodal, singleprofile, scgpt, resnet50, concat) is a clean single-variable comparison to config_009, changing only the RNA encoder from pca_n2000 to the learned scgpt representation. This isolates the effect of RNA representation quality while holding image encoder, fusion, and profile setting constant. Why this is the best next experiment: with only two data points, the most impactful uncertainty to resolve is whether a learned RNA encoder provides a meaningful gain over PCA in the multimodal pipeline. If scgpt substantially outperforms pca_n2000, it validates investing in learned representations and opens a high-performing branch for further exploration (multiprofile, aggregation, stronger image encoders). If the gain is marginal, it suggests the current PCA representation is sufficient and budget should shift to testing multiprofile or image encoder upgrades. This single-variable design maximizes information per experiment under the limited budget."
}

